==English==

===Etymology===
The ''[[lo]]'' from the expression likely originated from the shortening of the word ''[[loke]]'', commonly seen in {{etyl|enm}} texts. Its presence in literature can be traced as far back as the [[1800s]].  The literal meaning of the expression is "look and see", and it is always used as if in the imperative.

===Interjection===
{{en-interj|pos=[[lo]] [[and]] [[behold]]}}

# {{non-gloss definition|Used to express surprise.}}
#: ''And then, '''lo and behold''', she returned!''

====Usage notes====
* Often used ironically or humorously.

